// Debounce -- accept a noisy digital signal only once it has held still.
//
// A mechanical contact rings for a few milliseconds after every transition,
// and a rule that reads the pin directly sees every one of those edges. This
// makes the signal usable:
//
//   #Debounce btn  Settle=20
//   btn.In = !Button
//   Led = btn.Out
//
// Settle is in milliseconds and is an ordinary variable, so it stays patchable
// in the field -- which is what you want when a switch turns out to be worse
// than the datasheet claimed. It is counted on Tk, so Tk.period is the
// resolution and the accepted value can be up to one tick late.
//
// WHY A TICK AND A COUNTER rather than one timer held restarted: a running
// timer cannot be restarted. `T = 1` only arms a timer that has stopped, so
// "keep pushing the deadline away while the contact rings" cannot be written
// that way -- the timer runs its full period regardless and then samples
// whatever the input happens to read at that instant. That is a weaker filter
// (it is the manual's version), and if the ringing period lines up with the
// timer it can sample the same wrong edge every time. Counting milliseconds of
// stillness has no such phase to lock on to.
//
#module Debounce
#timer    Tk 10 = 1                 // resolution in ms -- also the tick
#variable In:1   unsigned in  = 0   // raw, bouncing
#variable Out:1  unsigned out = 0   // settled value
#variable Settle              = 50  // ms the input must hold still
#variable Hold                = 0   // ms In has kept its present value
#variable Was:1  unsigned     = 0   // In as of its last change

Tk = 1 ? timeout(Tk)

// Hold stops at Settle: nothing above it means anything different, and a
// button left alone for a month would otherwise reach integer overflow and
// come back around looking like a signal that had just moved.
Hold = Hold + Tk.period ? timeout(Tk)
Hold = Settle ? timeout(Tk) && Hold >= Settle
Hold = 0, Was = In ? In != Was

// `In == Was` is what makes the settle time real. On the cycle In moves, the
// reset above has not been committed yet, so Hold still holds the age of the
// PREVIOUS value -- which is normally well past Settle. Accepting on that would
// take every bounce edge immediately and the whole module would be decoration.
Out = In ? In == Was && Hold >= Settle
#end
